From 9dd2f7e8d785b0767d964985a3ffdc259c5949be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Andrei V. Lepikhov" <lepihov@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 20:12:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Hold small numeric sum accumulators inline in the aggregate
 state

NumericSumAccum palloc's its two digit buffers on first use, so a grouped
aggregate pays two allocations per group per aggregate.  Hold the digits in
buffers inside the struct while the accumulator is narrow enough for them, and
allocate only once it outgrows them; a numeric(15,2) column needs six digits,
so ordinary columns allocate nothing.

The inline size is bounded by the allocator, not by the digit count.  At eight
digits NumericAggState grows from 144 to 272 bytes and crosses from aset's
256-byte size class into the 512-byte one, doubling the state memory of a
large hash aggregate; that measured +9% on a 592k-group aggregate, swamping
anything the saved allocations return.  Six digits keeps the struct at 240
bytes and inside the original class.

Since the digit pointers may now refer to storage inside the struct, they must
not be pfree'd directly; accum_sum_free() encapsulates that, and
numeric_poly_stddev_internal() -- which builds a NumericAggState on the stack
-- has to go through it rather than freeing the buffers itself.

In passing, make accum_sum_copy() copy have_carry_space, which it was
omitting; the destination's buffer is the same width as the source's, so the
source's answer is the correct one, and without it a copied accumulator
enlarged itself once more than it needed to.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
